8 parameters, 6 targets to fit, 3 held out
   w_syn        [0.01, 1] log   published 0.275 (fitted there, against a female connectome); our earlier fit 0.075
   inh_gain     [0.5, 16] log   inhibition relative to excitation per synapse; balanced-network models commonly use 4-8
   tau_syn      [1, 15]   published 5 ms; ionotropic GABA and GABA-B differ by 10-100x, which one constant cannot represent
   v_th         [4, 12]   published threshold sits 7 mV above rest
   sigma        [0, 0.4]   background noise. Ours, not the published model's, which has none -- so it is fitted rather than assumed
   tau_mem      [10, 40]   published 20 ms, applied to every cell
   delay_ms     [0.8, 2]   the published 1.8 ms is one number for every connection, but measured giant-fibre latencies are 0.93-1.46 ms across one synapse and 1.44-1.85 ms across two, so a hop costs far less than the base. Bounded by those measurements
   tau_mem_kc   [150, 500]   Kenyon cells are measured at >200 ms, a lower bound. Searched below it too, so the fit can disagree with the measurement visibly rather than silently

